### What are the main use cases of Kafka?

Kafka serves as the event backbone for a wide range of use cases in modern distributed systems. Each use case takes advantage of the same core properties: partitioned topics for horizontal scale, configurable retention for replay, and consumer groups for independent parallel consumption. In banking, a single transfer event can simultaneously feed real-time fraud detection, audit logging, customer notifications, data warehousing, and disaster-recovery replication.

```java
package com.bank.events;

import org.springframework.kafka.core.KafkaTemplate;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class TransferEventPublisher {

    private final KafkaTemplate<String, String> kafkaTemplate;

    public TransferEventPublisher(KafkaTemplate<String, String> kafkaTemplate) {
        this.kafkaTemplate = kafkaTemplate;
    }

    public void publishTransfer(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        String event = "{\"accountId\":\"" + accountId
            + "\",\"amount\":" + amount + ",\"type\":\"TRANSFER\"}";
        kafkaTemplate.send("transactions", accountId, event);
        System.out.println("Published transfer event for: " + accountId);
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Published transfer event for: SAV2001
-> Fraud service consumes (fraud-group)
-> Audit service consumes (audit-group)
-> Notification service consumes (notification-group)
-> Analytics pipeline consumes (analytics-group)
```

### How does Kafka power real-time streaming?

Real-time streaming is Kafka's headline use case. Producers emit events and stream processors such as Kafka Streams or Apache Flink compute results continuously. In banking, a fraud engine reads the transaction stream and flags anomalies within milliseconds. The consumer processes each event as it arrives, maintaining a rolling state per account.

```java
package com.bank.fraud;

import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.KafkaListener;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;

@Component
public class RealTimeFraudDetector {

    private final Map<String, BigDecimal> rollingSum = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    private static final BigDecimal VELOCITY_LIMIT = new BigDecimal("500000.00");

    @KafkaListener(topics = "transactions", groupId = "fraud-group")
    public void detectFraud(String eventJson) {
        // Parse accountId and amount from event
        String accountId = "SAV2001"; // parsed from JSON
        BigDecimal amount = new BigDecimal("50000.00"); // parsed from JSON

        BigDecimal total = rollingSum.merge(accountId, amount, BigDecimal::add);
        if (total.compareTo(VELOCITY_LIMIT) > 0) {
            System.out.println("ALERT: Velocity limit exceeded for " + accountId
                + " total=" + total);
        } else {
            System.out.println("Transaction OK: " + accountId
                + " running total=" + total);
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Transaction OK: SAV2001 running total=50000.00
Transaction OK: SAV2001 running total=100000.00
...
ALERT: Velocity limit exceeded for SAV2001 total=550000.00
```

### How does Kafka handle log aggregation?

Log aggregation centralizes logs from hundreds of microservices into Kafka topics, from where they flow to Elasticsearch or a data lake. Instead of each microservice writing to its own file, every service produces structured log events to a shared `app-logs` topic. This decouples log producers from log storage and lets you switch backends without changing any service.

```java
package com.bank.logging;

import org.springframework.kafka.core.KafkaTemplate;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import java.time.Instant;

@Component
public class CentralizedLogger {

    private final KafkaTemplate<String, String> kafkaTemplate;

    public CentralizedLogger(KafkaTemplate<String, String> kafkaTemplate) {
        this.kafkaTemplate = kafkaTemplate;
    }

    public void log(String serviceName, String level, String message) {
        String logEvent = "{\"service\":\"" + serviceName
            + "\",\"level\":\"" + level
            + "\",\"message\":\"" + message
            + "\",\"timestamp\":\"" + Instant.now() + "\"}";
        kafkaTemplate.send("app-logs", serviceName, logEvent);
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output (Kafka -> Elasticsearch):
transfer-service -> app-logs topic -> Elasticsearch (logs-group)
fraud-service    -> app-logs topic -> Elasticsearch (logs-group)
payment-service  -> app-logs topic -> Elasticsearch (logs-group)
```

### How does Kafka enable event-driven microservices?

Event-driven microservices use Kafka as the integration backbone. Services do not call each other synchronously; they publish events and react to events. When the transfer service completes a transfer, it publishes a `TransferCompleted` event, and the notification and ledger services react independently. This removes tight coupling and allows services to evolve separately.

```java
package com.bank.notification;

import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.KafkaListener;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class NotificationService {

    @KafkaListener(topics = "transfer-completed", groupId = "notification-group")
    public void onTransferCompleted(String eventJson) {
        // Parse event and send SMS/email to customer
        System.out.println("Sending notification for transfer: " + eventJson);
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.ledger;

import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.KafkaListener;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class LedgerService {

    @KafkaListener(topics = "transfer-completed", groupId = "ledger-group")
    public void onTransferCompleted(String eventJson) {
        // Update the general ledger
        System.out.println("Updating ledger for transfer: " + eventJson);
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Transfer service publishes: transfer-completed
  -> NotificationService: Sending notification for transfer: {...}
  -> LedgerService: Updating ledger for transfer: {...}
```

### How does Change Data Capture (CDC) work with Kafka?

CDC streams row-level changes from a database into Kafka using tools like Debezium. Every insert, update, and delete on the accounts table becomes an event in a Kafka topic. This keeps caches, search indexes, and downstream systems in sync with the source database without dual writes, which avoids the consistency problems of writing to both a database and Kafka in application code.

```java
package com.bank.cdc;

import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.KafkaListener;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class AccountCdcConsumer {

    @KafkaListener(topics = "dbserver1.bank.accounts", groupId = "cache-sync-group")
    public void syncAccountCache(String cdcEventJson) {
        // Debezium CDC event contains before/after state of the row
        System.out.println("CDC event received: " + cdcEventJson);
        // Update Redis cache with latest account state
        System.out.println("Cache updated for account from CDC");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Database: UPDATE accounts SET balance = 75000 WHERE id = 'SAV2001'
Debezium captures change -> Kafka topic: dbserver1.bank.accounts
CDC event received: {"before":{"balance":50000},"after":{"balance":75000}}
Cache updated for account from CDC
```

### How does Kafka feed big-data pipelines?

Big-data pipelines use Kafka as the ingestion layer. Raw events land in Kafka, then Kafka Connect sinks them into HDFS, S3, or a warehouse like Snowflake for batch analytics. Kafka buffers massive spikes so the slower warehouse is not overwhelmed. This decouples the real-time event producers from the batch analytics consumers.

```java
package com.bank.pipeline;

public class BigDataPipelineOverview {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Big Data Pipeline Architecture:");
        System.out.println("1. Services produce events -> Kafka topics");
        System.out.println("2. Kafka Connect S3 Sink -> S3 bucket (Parquet)");
        System.out.println("3. Snowflake reads from S3 -> analytics queries");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Kafka buffers 1M events/sec during peak hours.");
        System.out.println("S3 sink writes in 5-minute batches.");
        System.out.println("No events lost, warehouse never overwhelmed.");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Big Data Pipeline Architecture:
1. Services produce events -> Kafka topics
2. Kafka Connect S3 Sink -> S3 bucket (Parquet)
3. Snowflake reads from S3 -> analytics queries

Kafka buffers 1M events/sec during peak hours.
S3 sink writes in 5-minute batches.
No events lost, warehouse never overwhelmed.
```

### How does Kafka handle cross-datacenter replication?

Replication and synchronization across datacenters use MirrorMaker 2 to copy topics from one Kafka cluster to another. This enables disaster recovery and multi-region active-active setups. If the Mumbai datacenter fails, the Chennai datacenter already has every event replicated and can take over processing.

```java
package com.bank.replication;

public class MirrorMakerOverview {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Cross-Datacenter Replication with MirrorMaker 2:");
        System.out.println("Mumbai cluster (primary):");
        System.out.println("  transfer-events: 6 partitions, RF=3");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Chennai cluster (DR replica):");
        System.out.println("  mumbai.transfer-events: mirrored topic");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("MirrorMaker 2 copies records in near-real-time.");
        System.out.println("If Mumbai fails, Chennai consumers switch topics.");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Cross-Datacenter Replication with MirrorMaker 2:
Mumbai cluster (primary):
  transfer-events: 6 partitions, RF=3

Chennai cluster (DR replica):
  mumbai.transfer-events: mirrored topic

MirrorMaker 2 copies records in near-real-time.
If Mumbai fails, Chennai consumers switch topics.
```

### What about content delivery and fan-out?

Content delivery and fan-out push the same content or notification to many consumers efficiently. A single "new statement available" event fans out to email, SMS, and push-notification consumers. Each consumer group processes the event independently, so adding a new notification channel is just adding a new consumer group.

```java
package com.bank.notification;

import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.KafkaListener;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class MultiChannelNotifier {

    @KafkaListener(topics = "statement-available", groupId = "email-group")
    public void sendEmail(String event) {
        System.out.println("Email sent for statement: " + event);
    }

    @KafkaListener(topics = "statement-available", groupId = "sms-group")
    public void sendSms(String event) {
        System.out.println("SMS sent for statement: " + event);
    }

    @KafkaListener(topics = "statement-available", groupId = "push-group")
    public void sendPush(String event) {
        System.out.println("Push notification sent: " + event);
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output (one event, three channels):
Email sent for statement: {"accountId":"SAV2001","month":"2025-01"}
SMS sent for statement: {"accountId":"SAV2001","month":"2025-01"}
Push notification sent: {"accountId":"SAV2001","month":"2025-01"}
```

### What should a senior engineer know about Kafka use cases?

The senior insight is that all these use cases share the same three Kafka properties applied differently. Partitioning gives horizontal scale, retention gives replay, and consumer groups give independent parallel consumption. Once you internalize that, every Kafka use case is the same tool pointed at a different problem. The key design decision is choosing the right topic structure, key strategy, and retention policy for each use case.

```java
package com.bank.architecture;

public class KafkaUseCaseSummary {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("All Kafka use cases share three core properties:");
        System.out.println("1. Partitioning  -> horizontal scale");
        System.out.println("2. Retention     -> replay capability");
        System.out.println("3. Consumer groups -> independent consumption");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Use Case            | Key Property Used");
        System.out.println("Real-time streaming | Partitioning (parallel processing)");
        System.out.println("Log aggregation     | Retention (long-term storage)");
        System.out.println("Event-driven micro  | Consumer groups (independence)");
        System.out.println("CDC                 | All three (sync, replay, parallel)");
        System.out.println("Big-data pipeline   | Retention (buffering spikes)");
        System.out.println("Replication (DR)    | Retention + MirrorMaker 2");
        System.out.println("Content fan-out     | Consumer groups (multi-channel)");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
All Kafka use cases share three core properties:
1. Partitioning  -> horizontal scale
2. Retention     -> replay capability
3. Consumer groups -> independent consumption

Use Case            | Key Property Used
Real-time streaming | Partitioning (parallel processing)
Log aggregation     | Retention (long-term storage)
Event-driven micro  | Consumer groups (independence)
CDC                 | All three (sync, replay, parallel)
Big-data pipeline   | Retention (buffering spikes)
Replication (DR)    | Retention + MirrorMaker 2
Content fan-out     | Consumer groups (multi-channel)
```
